*combine* | V-$CHIPVR ($CHIPCN)

Chippy's bundler and build system

*Usage:*

- ''chippy combine''                          Bundle a project
- ''chippy combine <config>''                 Bundle a project with an explicit combine config file
- ''chippy combine new''                      Create a new combine.chp file

- ''chippy combine test''                     Run the project's test suite
- ''chippy combine test <file>''              Run the tests in one test file
- ''chippy combine test <pattern>''           Run the tests whose names match a pattern
- ''chippy combine test <file> <pattern>''    Run the matching tests in one test file
- ''chippy combine <config> test ...''        Any of the above with an explicit combine config file

- ''chippy combine run''                      Run the project without writing a bundle
- ''chippy combine run <args...>''            Run the project with command line arguments
- ''chippy combine <config> run <args...>''   Any of the above with an explicit combine config file

*Configuration:*

- ''Project'': Output program name
- ''Version'': Program version string
- ''Licence'': Path to license file to include
- ''Output'': Output executable filename
- ''Source'': Main source file entry point
- ''Paths'': List of directories to search for dependencies
- ''External'': List of dependencies combine shouldn't bundle and just keep as loads
- ''StripComments'': Remove comments from bundled output
- ''StripWhitespace'': Remove excess whitespace from bundled output
- ''Compile'': Write the bundle as bytecode instead of source
- ''AddShebang'': Add ''#!/usr/bin/chippy'' shebang to output, source or bytecode

*What it does:*

- Automatic dependency resolution via ''load(filename)'' statements
- Circular dependency prevention
- Licence header insertion (when configured)
- Comment/whitespace stripping (when configured)
- Safety checks:
	- Symbol collision detection in global scope (see ''chippy doc scoping'')
	- Syntax error detection

*Source bundles and compiled bundles:*

With ''Compile'' set to ''false'', combine writes one Chippy source file. You can read it, run it, see documentation it with ''chippy doc'', and bundle it again as part of a larger program.

With ''Compile'' set to ''true'', combine bundles the sources exactly the same way and then compiles the result to bytecode. The program starts up faster since it does not need to be compiled from source at runtime, but in this state it is a finished program, so its documentation is stripped, and it cannot be bundled again.

''StripComments'' and ''StripWhitespace'' only affect source bundles. Comments and spacing are not part of the bytecode.

''AddShebang'' applies to both source bundles as well as compiled bytecode bundles.

Dependencies listed in ''External'' stay as ''load(filename)'' calls in both cases, and are resolved when the program runs.

*Running without bundling:*

''chippy combine run'' resolves the exact same dependency graph as a regular bundle build, and then runs the program. It runs every dependency in topological order, under their own paths. After this, it runs the file at ''Source''.

This only honours the options ''Source'', ''Paths'' and ''External'', as nothing is written to disk.

Because each file runs under its real path, RTErrors point at the actual files and lines on disk instead of a generated bundle. This makes it somewhat better for debugging.

*Testing*

Please see ''chippy doc testing''.

*Returns:*

- Exit code 0 on success, non-zero on failure

*Recommended Reading*

''chippy doc testing''
''chippy doc chpdoc''
